    "submission": "submission-review-durable.json",
    "summary": "Turn 23 reviewed the first check for the stable-store metadata claim, the last unreviewed result.\n\nState on entry. The new claim C2-durable-record-preserves-written-fields has one artifact (efdc2a8c04df42ad93bf7d4f66be08ec, executed as 310d33db…) with outcome violated and oracle correspondence unreviewed; the recovery claim's four artifacts remain as they were, three of them confirmed.\n\nReview judgement (checker_correspondence, no_issue_found). Requirement versus predicate: the obligation is that each field the recorder writes is recoverable at the offset where the writer placed it, and the predicate asserts bal_field_present on the header event; the retained run shows the header verbatim in hex with bal_field_present false, vbal_at_0 true, status_at_4 true and seq_at_5 true, so the derived boolean is checkable against the printed bytes and the written values. Entry and caller duty: the recorder runs only with Durable true, which the harness sets and the prerequisite write-request event reports together with the instance identity and the field values; sync is called as production callers do, and no further caller duty is needed for a one-record premise. Oracle: comparison is against the value the harness asked for, with distinct nonzero values so a zeroed buffer cannot pass by accident, and the Deps region is excluded rather than silently assumed. Identity: the write request and the header event share instance 0.0, so correlation is through the write under test rather than array order. Constructed input and legality: the claim's applicability is exactly one recorder call with Durable true and distinct bal/vbal/Status/Seq, so a constructed instance is the full premise, and the alternative reading recorded in the claim (the layout deliberately stores vbal in place of bal) is named as what a reviewer would have to accept instead, which would make both the bal write and the 9+4N sizing vestigial. Observation path and completion: write, sync, close, read back, then emit, with no wait, join or cleanup duty.\n\nLimits kept explicit: no reader of the stable store exists in the snapshot, so the intended offsets are inferred from the recorder's own writes and buffer size; the shipped run.go passes durable=false, so this is an implementation-semantics defect rather than a live failure in the default deployment; and only one instance and record are observed, leaving repeated records, the Deps region and partial writes uncompared. No map object, requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened."
